ABOUT THE PROJECT
--------------------------------------------------
The Blackjack Simulator is developed using Python.
It is divided into different modules that handle
game mechanics, betting strategies, simulations,
and statistical analysis.


REPOSITORY STRUCTURE
--------------------------------------------------
blackjack_lt1/
    |-- blackjack/
    |-- notebooks/
    |-- presentation/
    |-- tests/
    |-- .flake8
    |-- .gitignore
    |-- Makefile
    |-- README.md
    |-- VERIFICATION.md
    |-- blackjack_rules
    |-- payout_structures
    |-- pyproject.toml
    |-- repo_technical_structure
    |-- requirements.txt
    |-- strategies.txt

Main directories:

blackjack/    - Main source code of the program.
notebooks/    - Demonstrations and data analysis.
presentation/ - Project presentation materials.
tests/        - Automated program testing.

Configuration files:

requirements.txt - Required Python libraries.
pyproject.toml   - Project and tool configurations.
Makefile         - Automates development tasks.
.flake8          - Code quality checking settings.
.gitignore       - Excludes unnecessary files from Git.

Documentation and user guides:

README.md                - Project information and instructions.
VERIFICATION.md          - Project verification details.
blackjack_rules          - Explains the rules of Blackjack.
payout_structures        - Explains payouts for different outcomes.
repo_technical_structure - Explains the repository structure, Python modules, and technical components.
strategies.txt           - Explains the different playing and betting strategies used in the program.

MAIN PYTHON MODULES
--------------------------------------------------
cards.py      - Card and deck management.
hand.py       - Calculation of hand values.
rules.py      - Blackjack game rules.
engine.py     - Game mechanics and round outcomes.
strategy.py   - Basic strategy decisions.
counting.py   - Hi-Lo card counting.
analytics.py  - Statistical analysis of game results.
bots.py       - Automated playing and betting strategies.
simulation.py - Repeated game simulations.
charts.py     - Data visualization.
cli.py        - Command-line user interface.


LIBRARIES AND DEVELOPMENT TOOLS
--------------------------------------------------
NumPy    - Numerical calculations, simulation arrays,
           and graph preparation (simulation.py, charts.py).
Pandas   - Data organization and analysis
           (listed in requirements.txt).
Plotly   - Interactive graphs and visualizations
           (charts.py).
Rich     - Terminal interface and formatted output
           (cli.py).
Pytest   - Automated testing of program functionality
           (tests/).
Jupyter  - Interactive demonstrations and analysis
           (notebooks/Prog_Project.ipynb).
Black    - Python code formatting.
Flake8   - Code quality and style checking.
nbformat - Jupyter Notebook file handling.
nbclient - Notebook execution and verification.
ipykernel - Python execution within Jupyter Notebook.
Built-in Python modules:
dataclasses - Organizes structured data.
typing      - Provides type annotations.

Both are used in analytics.py.
External dependencies are listed in requirements.txt.

GENERAL PROGRAM FLOW
--------------------------------------------------
Interactive Gameplay:

    User Input (cli.py)
        |
        v
    Game Engine (engine.py)
        |
        v
    Cards, Hands, Rules and Strategies
        |
        v
    Game Results

Simulation and Analysis:

    Automated Players (bots.py)
        |
        v
    Game Simulations (simulation.py)
        |
        v
    Statistical Analysis (analytics.py)
        |
        v
    Charts and Output (charts.py)

The tests/ directory contains automated tests that
verify the functionality and correctness of the
program's components.

PURPOSE OF THE MODULAR STRUCTURE
--------------------------------------------------
The project separates its functions into different
Python files to make the program more organized.

This allows developers to modify, test, and debug
individual components without unnecessarily
affecting other parts of the program.

It also improves code readability, reusability,
maintainability, and collaboration among developers.

